  "headline": "An oil-led rate shock, not a growth boom: yields at highs while gold and copper buckle",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is stagflationary pressure being priced through the bond market. Brent near $99 is up 11% on the month and 63% year to date, and Treasury yields have followed: the 10-year at 5.24% is up 28 basis points this week and 52 over the month, the 30-year at 5.56% up 27 and 36, both at 52-week highs. That the 10-year is rising faster than the 30-year, flattening the 30y–10y spread by 17 basis points over the month, says the market is pricing a tighter policy path to contain energy-led inflation rather than stronger long-run growth.",
    "Gold confirms the mechanism. Down 3.6% today, 5% on the week and 8% on the month, it is being squeezed by higher real yields, not bid as a haven — this is a rates story, not a fear story. The copper/gold ratio's 10% monthly rise and bitcoin's 12% gain against gold flatter the growth and liquidity read; both are driven almost entirely by gold's collapse. Copper down 2.7% and bitcoin down 3.7% this week say risk appetite is fading under the rate pressure.",
    "The tension is that copper still sits at 87% of its range, up 18% year to date, and bitcoin is up 40% over three months — growth and liquidity had not broken until recently. This week's uniform direction outweighs that. The dollar index, up 0.8% on the week near its 52-week high, and USD/CAD up 2.2% on the month despite $99 oil, show yield differentials, not commodity strength, are setting currencies."
  ],
